{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposal fails to demonstrate that would address&nbsp;an identified need, be inclusive and accessible,&nbsp;and be appropriately and sustainably located with a range of travel options. The provision of a new educational facility in this location is therefore at odds with the following policies which seek to ensure that proposals for new social infrastructure meet an identified need and are appropriately located:\r\n National Planning Policy Framework (December 2023)\r\n Policies GG1, S1 and S3 of the London Plan (March 2021)\r\n Policies CM1 and CC2 of the&nbsp;Local Development Framework Core\r\n     Strategy (July 2010)\r\n Policies SP2 and SP4 of&nbsp;The London Borough of\r\n     Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission\r\n     Version, December 2021)\r\n",
    "The proposed development including ancillary features, by reason of siting, scale, design and use, represents inappropriate, inaccessible development which is at odds with the site’s established arrangement as a residential dwelling and&nbsp;would appear disproportionate to the scale of the retained building, constituting overdevelopment of the plot. It is therefore at odds with the following policies which seek to ensure that development proposals have a high-quality, inclusive design which is respectful of the surrounding character and appearance:\r\n National Planning Policy Framework (December 2023)\r\n Policies D4 and D5 London Plan (March 2021)\r\n Policy CP3 of the&nbsp;Local Development Framework Core\r\n     Strategy (July 2010)\r\n Policies&nbsp;BP8 and BP11 of the&nbsp;Local Development\r\n     Framework Borough Wide Development Plan Document (March 2011)\r\n Policies SP2 and DMD1 of&nbsp;The London Borough of\r\n     Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission\r\n     Version, December 2021)\r\n",
    "The proposed development&nbsp;would result in unneighbourly levels of noise generation, increased comings-and-goings and general disturbances, and a loss of privacy to neighbouring dwellings, to the extent that this would constitute undue harm to the living conditions of neighbouring occupiers. The proposal is therefore found to be at odds with the\r\nfollowing policies which seek to ensure that development proposals adequately\r\nprotect residential amenity:\r\n\r\n\r\n National Planning Policy Framework (December 2023)\r\n Policies GG3, D6 (part D) and D14 of the London Plan (March 2021)\r\n Policies BR13, BP8 and BP11 of the&nbsp;Local Development\r\n     Framework Borough Wide Development Plan Document (March 2011)\r\n Policies DMD1 and DMSI3 of&nbsp;The London Borough of\r\n     Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission\r\n     Version, December 2021)\r\n",
    "The proposed development is not&nbsp;sustainably located with a range of travel options, failing to support sustainable modes of transport. In the absence of further information, it is likely that the proposal would result in increased comings-and-goings by car, resulting in increased vehicular movements and parking pressure within the surrounding area. As such, the development is contrary to:&nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (December 2023)Policies T1, T2, T4 and T6 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policy CP3 of the&nbsp;Local Development\r\nFramework Core Strategy (July 2010)Policies BR9, BR10, BR11 and BP11 of the&nbsp;Local Development Framework Borough Wide Development Plan Document (March 2011)Policies SP8, DMT1 and DMT2 of&nbsp;The London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Draft Local Plan: (Regulation 19 Submission Version, December 2021)"
  ],
